WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.493 --> 00:00:05.308
Ask an image generator for a dog to the right of a tie.

2
00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.063
It looks great, but the tie is on the wrong side.

3
00:00:08.393 --> 00:00:11.243
Language models learned to check and correct their own work.

4
00:00:11.425 --> 00:00:13.465
Image generators still render once.

5
00:00:13.793 --> 00:00:17.187
So, can one model look at its own image, think, and fix it?

6
00:00:19.421 --> 00:00:21.612
Prior work uses an external critic,

7
00:00:21.666 --> 00:00:24.250
single-round RL that never sees its result,

8
00:00:24.432 --> 00:00:27.561
or imitation that copies the format, not the fix.

9
00:00:27.909 --> 00:00:30.690
Unified models like BAGEL can already look and draw.

10
00:00:30.872 --> 00:00:33.595
What's missing is the behavior: native reflection.

11
00:00:33.938 --> 00:00:36.256
The model draws an image, then looks at it,

12
00:00:36.438 --> 00:00:39.531
and writes a reflection: the round, a self-score,

13
00:00:39.678 --> 00:00:41.313
what's wrong, and an action.

14
00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:46.218
Edit, and it redraws; done, and it stops, within three rounds.

15
00:00:46.411 --> 00:00:49.956
Text and images share one sequence and one set of weights.

16
00:00:50.788 --> 00:00:53.074
Step one, supervised fine-tuning,

17
00:00:53.114 --> 00:00:58.025
on reflection trajectories from a GPT-5.5 critic and separate editors.

18
00:00:58.371 --> 00:00:59.682
The format is learned:

19
00:00:59.783 --> 00:01:01.985
95% of outputs follow it.

20
00:01:02.179 --> 00:01:04.659
But only one in five wrong images gets fixed.

21
00:01:04.841 --> 00:01:07.830
It moves the tie, then the dog, then the tie again.

22
00:01:08.188 --> 00:01:10.251
Step two, whole-trajectory RL.

23
00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:11.698
From one initial image,

24
00:01:11.741 --> 00:01:14.626
we sample 16 full reflection trajectories.

25
00:01:14.971 --> 00:01:16.212
These are real siblings.

26
00:01:16.406 --> 00:01:17.578
Some fix it in round one.

27
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.488
Some need two or three rounds.

28
00:01:19.682 --> 00:01:20.750
Some go in circles.

29
00:01:20.932 --> 00:01:23.192
And one gets worse, before it recovers.

30
00:01:23.526 --> 00:01:27.268
A frozen detector scores every image, with graded confidences,

31
00:01:27.311 --> 00:01:29.085
so partial fixes still count.

32
00:01:29.426 --> 00:01:33.793
Each trajectory earns its final score, plus progress, minus damage.

33
00:01:33.975 --> 00:01:36.663
Then siblings are compared: subtract the mean,

34
00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:40.019
divide by the spread, and clip to plus or minus one.

35
00:01:40.343 --> 00:01:43.343
Better than average is reinforced; worse is suppressed.

36
00:01:43.525 --> 00:01:45.534
All siblings start from the same image,

37
00:01:45.574 --> 00:01:48.413
so only the reflections and edits make the difference.

38
00:01:48.754 --> 00:01:51.605
One advantage is shared by every reflection token,

39
00:01:51.682 --> 00:01:53.537
and the selected denoising steps,

40
00:01:53.581 --> 00:01:55.806
so text and image learn together.

41
00:01:55.1000 --> 00:01:58.098
The detector is only used in training.

42
00:01:59.443 --> 00:02:02.062
After RL, the model pinpoints the problem,

43
00:02:02.139 --> 00:02:03.497
and fixes it in round one.

44
00:02:03.826 --> 00:02:07.336
On GenEval, all three models start just above 70.

45
00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:10.264
Without RL, reflection adds a point or two.

46
00:02:10.446 --> 00:02:14.269
After RL, round one alone adds 9, reaching 84.

47
00:02:14.593 --> 00:02:18.577
Of the images that start out wrong, SFT fixes 20%.

48
00:02:18.759 --> 00:02:20.371
RL fixes 65%.

49
00:02:20.709 --> 00:02:24.393
And the gains transfer to WISE, OneIG, and CompBench,

50
00:02:24.471 --> 00:02:25.608
unseen in training.

51
00:02:26.943 --> 00:02:28.450
At the same four-image budget,

52
00:02:28.598 --> 00:02:32.027
letting the model pick the best of four samples scores 78.

53
00:02:32.209 --> 00:02:34.550
Reflecting on one image scores 84.

54
00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:38.687
Where all four samples fail, RL still recovers 60%;

55
00:02:38.881 --> 00:02:40.400
SFT, 12%.

56
00:02:41.526 --> 00:02:42.536
Inside the model,

57
00:02:42.672 --> 00:02:45.430
a linear probe already tells whether an image is right,

58
00:02:45.635 --> 00:02:48.647
equally well for Base, SFT, and RL.

59
00:02:48.993 --> 00:02:50.755
What changes is where the images go.

60
00:02:50.925 --> 00:02:52.711
Of the images that start out wrong,

61
00:02:52.766 --> 00:02:57.561
the share inside the passing region grows from 34% to 62% with RL,

62
00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:00.628
but only from 36% to 42% with SFT.

63
00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:03.196
RL doesn't rebuild the model's eyes.

64
00:03:03.378 --> 00:03:06.067
It teaches the model to act on what it already knows.

65
00:03:10.109 --> 00:03:12.821
SFT teaches the format of reflection.

66
00:03:13.014 --> 00:03:15.448
Whole-trajectory RL turns it into repair.

67
00:03:15.776 --> 00:03:20.559
Learning to correct itself may be a new way for visual generation to scale at test time.

68
00:03:20.764 --> 00:03:21.578
Thanks for watching.
